1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
Two groups of order 4. Both satisfy every axiom. Both have four elements. Are they the same group?

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,000
On the left, the integers 0 to 3 under addition mod 4. On the right, pairs of bits added component by component.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,000
The axioms cannot separate them. Everything the axioms demand is true of both. Only the tables differ.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:24,000
A relabelling is a bijection that also respects the table: phi of a plus b equals phi of a plus phi of b.

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Z4 has one element of order 2. V4 has three. A relabelling preserves order, so no relabelling exists.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,000
Two groups are isomorphic when a bijection exists that preserves the operation. The bijection is the definition.

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:42,000
Three things come free: the identity goes to the identity, inverses to inverses, and the order of every element is preserved.

8
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:48,000
Isomorphism is an equivalence relation. Reflexive by the identity map, symmetric by inverting the bijection, transitive by composing two of them.

9
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Checked here on four groups of order 6: reflexive 4 of 4, symmetric on all 12 ordered pairs, transitive on all 64 ordered triples.

10
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
So isomorphic means: the same group with the elements renamed. Every question answerable from the table has the same answer in both.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Now the other direction. Claim: Z6 and Z2 x Z3 are the same group. The proof is a map.

12
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:12,000
The candidate is phi of x equals x mod 2 paired with x mod 3. Send each number to its two remainders.

13
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Thirty-six ordered pairs to check. A pair a and b is fine when phi of a plus b equals phi of a plus phi of b.

14
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Zero violations in thirty-six. And the order of each element is preserved, six of six, which the definition guarantees.

15
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:30,000
So the two are one group. A proof of sameness is always a witness; a proof of difference is always an invariant.

16
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:36,000
An invariant is a number you can compute from the table that a relabelling cannot change. Six of them are enough for this lesson.

17
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:42,000
One: the order of the group. Two: whether it is abelian. Three: the element-order profile, which is how many elements have each order.

18
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:48,000
Four: the size of the centre. Five: how many subgroups of each order. Six: the automorphism count, which is the number of relabellings of a group onto itself.

19
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:54,000
A mismatch in any one of them proves the groups differ. That direction is airtight, and it is all an exam answer needs.

20
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:00,000
A match proves nothing yet. Every one of these is necessary, and the next scenes show that none of them is sufficient.

21
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:06,000
Order 4, the first real case. Z4 on the left, V4 on the right. Both abelian, so the abelianness test is useless here.

22
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:12,000
In Z4 the element orders are 1, 4, 2, 4. One element has order 2, two have order 4, and the identity has order 1.

23
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:18,000
In V4 every non-identity element has order 2. The profile is one of order 1 and three of order 2, with nothing above.

24
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:24,000
One against three. A bijection sends elements of order 2 to elements of order 2, so this one cannot exist.

25
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
The search agrees and never tests a product: six bijections fix the identity, the profile filter removes all six, zero survive.

26
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Order 6. Z6 against S3, the permutations of three objects. Six elements each, so the first invariant fails.

27
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:42,000
Z6 is abelian: a plus b equals b plus a for all thirty-six pairs. Every subgroup of an abelian group is normal.

28
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:48,000
S3 is not. The transposition 0 1 and the three-cycle 0 1 2 give different answers in the two orders.

29
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:54,000
Abelianness is preserved by every isomorphism, so one line settles it. The profile agrees, and says it a second way.

30
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:00,000
The search: 120 bijections fix the identity, the profile filter removes all of them, and no product is tested.

31
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:06,000
Order 8. Now the cheap invariants run out. D4 against Q8: both non-abelian, both with a centre of size 2.

32
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:12,000
In D4 the element orders are 1, 4, 2, 4, 2, 2, 2, 2. Two elements of order 4 and five of order 2.

33
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:18,000
In Q8 the element orders are 1, 4, 2, 4, 2, 4, 2, 4. Six elements of order 4 and only one of order 2.

34
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:24,000
Five against one. The profiles disagree, so D4 and Q8 are not isomorphic, and nothing else had to be computed.

35
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:30,000
The subgroup counts agree here, three of order 4 each, and the automorphism counts do not: 8 for D4 against 24 for Q8.

36
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:36,000
There is a test that always works. Try every bijection that sends the identity to the identity, and check every product.

37
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:42,000
Order 4 costs 6 bijections and 96 product checks. Order 6 costs 120 and 4320. Order 8 costs 5040 and 322560.

38
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:48,000
Now prune it. Skip any candidate that sends an element of order d to an element of a different order.

39
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:54,000
D4 against Q8: 5040 candidates become zero before a single product is tested. The profile test is the search.

40
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:00,000
Z6 against Z2 x Z3: four candidates survive the filter, and two of them pass the product test. Those two are the isomorphisms.

41
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:06,000
Now the trap. Two groups of order 16 on this page have the same element-order profile and are not the same group.

42
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:12,000
Z4 x Z4 and Q8 x Z2: both order 16, both with 1 element of order 1, 3 of order 2 and 12 of order 4.

43
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:18,000
But one is abelian and the other is not. Two groups with the same profile, and no isomorphism between them.

44
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:24,000
So a matching profile is never a proof of sameness. It removes candidates. It does not produce a map.

45
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:30,000
A mismatching invariant is always a proof of difference. A matching one never is. That single sentence is the topic.

46
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:36,000
The procedure, cheapest first: compare the order, then abelianness, then the element-order profile, then write the map.

47
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:42,000
A question that asks how many non-isomorphic groups of a given order exist is a classification question, not a comparison.

48
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:48,000
Order 4 has two classes: Z4 and V4. So a question asking for the number of non-isomorphic abelian groups of order 4 has answer 2.

49
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:54,000
Order 6 has two classes, order 8 has five, and order 9 has two. Every group of prime order is cyclic, so prime orders have one.

50
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Sameness needs a witness. Difference needs an invariant. Decide which one the question is asking before you compute anything.

